verable through filesystem snapshots\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor built an LVM-backed XFS filesystem for /srv/data (loop-backed VG since no free block device/partition existed) and demonstrated the snapshot mechanism end-to-end: baseline data populated, LVM snapshot data_snap1 created, and later merged back to restore exact content. This is a valid, standard implementation of filesystem-snapshot-based recoverability given the constraints (no btrfs support, no free partition).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e56a65fd4fcf4479\",\"cmd-700fa9f2f09c4c81\",\"cmd-aa97bd66d9274262\",\"cmd-8ff3437981de4922\",\"cmd-83a385c54b2344d5\",\"cmd-7ca53c91e15b47cf\",\"cmd-f20a739276a4439e\"]},{\"id\":\"modify-after-snapshot\",\"requirement\":\"Directory modified after the snapshot is taken\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After creating snapshot data_snap1, executor changed settings.conf content, appended to data.db, deleted app.log, and added a junk file, with file listing confirming divergence from baseline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c592b5a6344b46d7\"]},{\"id\":\"restore-exact-snapshot\",\"requirement\":\"Restore the exact content captured by the earlier snapshot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"lvconvert --merge completed to 100%, volume remounted, and sha256sum -c against the pre-modification baseline manifest reported OK for all three original files; the junk file and appended data were gone, confirming exact restoration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6b288e8d1c2f447b\",\"cmd-816893268c3647f9\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id d052eb57... and healthy checksums captured, systemctl reboot issued and acknowledged by connection drop, post-restart boot_id 56057518... differs, and srv-data-loop.service/loop device/VG/LV/mount and data checksums were all confirmed recovered automatically after reboot. Cross-checked against fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot which independently shows the same boot_id change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-caa4d2159c414f9d\",\"cmd-f392b078122f4d2e\",\"cmd-b9b8000eda2f44f2\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Reviewed all state-changing executor commands chronologically: loop file/PV/VG/LV creation, XFS format, mount, systemd unit + fstab entry for persistence, baseline data population, snapshot creation, data modification (part of the demonstrated workflow), unmount/merge/remount, and the reboot. Every mutation has a clear causal role in either building the snapshot-capable filesystem, demonstrating the modify/restore cycle, or completing the persistence needed for the restart protocol to succeed. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations were found; no failed or conflicting services were left behind (failed-services observation is empty pre- and post-executor). The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, an incidental side effect of the reboot command itself, which is negligible. Repeated 'file descriptor leaked' warnings from lvm2 commands are informational stderr noise from the tool itself, not evidence of a state-changing mistake.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3a0ed8e0320345fc\",\"cmd-e56a65fd4fcf4479\",\"cmd-700fa9f2f09c4c81\",\"cmd-aa97bd66d9274262\",\"cmd-8ff3437981de4922\",\"cmd-83a385c54b2344d5\",\"cmd-3c3a61c6e69a4faa\",\"cmd-76e7811177174f16\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the public task contract were directly demonstrated: an LVM/XFS-based snapshot mechanism was built for /srv/data, the directory was modified post-snapshot, and the exact pre-modification content was restored via lvconvert --merge with SHA256 verification. The independent node-restart protocol was also fully and coherently demonstrated, with matching boot-identity evidence in both the executor timeline and the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and full recovery of the loop device, LVM stack, mount, and data confirmed post-reboot. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were found; hygiene is high.\",\"limitations\":[\"The root disk had no free partition/LVM/btrfs support, so the snapshot filesystem was loop-file-backed rather than on dedicated block storage; this is a reasonable workaround under the topology's constraints but is a less production-typical setup than a dedicated LV/partition.\",\"Lifecycle collector treated after_prepare as an alias of before_prepare since prepare was disabled, so preparer-vs-executor separation relies entirely on the executor timeline rather than a distinct after_prepare snapshot.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"snapshot-capable-storage","requirement":"Application data under /srv/data on node1 kept recoverable through filesystem snapshots","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor built an LVM-backed XFS filesystem for /srv/data (loop-backed VG since no free block device/partition existed) and demonstrated the snapshot mechanism end-to-end: baseline data populated, LVM snapshot data_snap1 created, and later merged back to restore exact content. This is a valid, standard implementation of filesystem-snapshot-based recoverability given the constraints (no btrfs support, no free partition).","evidence_ids":["cmd-e56a65fd4fcf4479","cmd-700fa9f2f09c4c81","cmd-aa97bd66d9274262","cmd-8ff3437981de4922","cmd-83a385c54b2344d5","cmd-7ca53c91e15b47cf","cmd-f20a739276a4439e"]},{"id":"modify-after-snapshot","requirement":"Directory modified after the snapshot is taken","status":"satisfied","summary":"After creating snapshot data_snap1, executor changed settings.conf content, appended to data.db, deleted app.log, and added a junk file, with file listing confirming divergence from baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c592b5a6344b46d7"]},{"id":"restore-exact-snapshot","requirement":"Restore the exact content captured by the earlier snapshot","status":"satisfied","summary":"lvconvert --merge completed to 100%, volume remounted, and sha256sum -c against the pre-modification baseline manifest reported OK for all three original files; the junk file and appended data were gone, confirming exact restoration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6b288e8d1c2f447b","cmd-816893268c3647f9"]},{"id":"node-restart-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered state)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot_id d052eb57... and healthy checksums captured, systemctl reboot issued and acknowledged by connection drop, post-restart boot_id 56057518... differs, and srv-data-loop.service/loop device/VG/LV/mount and data checksums were all confirmed recovered automatically after reboot. Cross-checked against fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot which independently shows the same boot_id change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-caa4d2159c414f9d","cmd-f392b078122f4d2e","cmd-b9b8000eda2f44f2","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Reviewed all state-changing executor commands chronologically: loop file/PV/VG/LV creation, XFS format, mount, systemd unit + fstab entry for persistence, baseline data population, snapshot creation, data modification (part of the demonstrated workflow), unmount/merge/remount, and the reboot. Every mutation has a clear causal role in either building the snapshot-capable filesystem, demonstrating the modify/restore cycle, or completing the persistence needed for the restart protocol to succeed. No unnecessary or unrelated mutations were found; no failed or conflicting services were left behind (failed-services observation is empty pre- and post-executor). The only residue is /tmp/reboot.log, an incidental side effect of the reboot command itself, which is negligible. Repeated 'file descriptor leaked' warnings from lvm2 commands are informational stderr noise from the tool itself, not evidence of a state-changing mistake.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3a0ed8e0320345fc","cmd-e56a65fd4fcf4479","cmd-700fa9f2f09c4c81","cmd-aa97bd66d9274262","cmd-8ff3437981de4922","cmd-83a385c54b2344d5","cmd-3c3a61c6e69a4faa","cmd-76e7811177174f16","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the public task contract were directly demonstrated: an LVM/XFS-based snapshot mechanism was built for /srv/data, the directory was modified post-snapshot, and the exact pre-modification content was restored via lvconvert --merge with SHA256 verification. The independent node-restart protocol was also fully and coherently demonstrated, with matching boot-identity evidence in both the executor timeline and the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and full recovery of the loop device, LVM stack, mount, and data confirmed post-reboot. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were found; hygiene is high.","limitations":["The root disk had no free partition/LVM/btrfs support, so the snapshot filesystem was loop-file-backed rather than on dedicated block storage; this is a reasonable workaround under the topology's constraints but is a less production-typical setup than a dedicated LV/partition.","Lifecycle collector treated after_prepare as an alias of before_prepare since prepare was disabled, so preparer-vs-executor separation relies entirely on the executor timeline rather than a distinct after_prepare snapshot."]},"ttft_ms":6899,"type":"result","duration_ms":43979,"uuid":"7c49443c-02da-4d78-8e9b-9804376a01f1","ttft_stream_ms":1375,"time_to_request_ms":48,"first_content_frame_ms":1989,"queued_turn_count":0}
